but first the latest on our top story  president Trump is using this weekend to  narrow his choices of Supreme Court  picks CBS News has learned there are two  leading contenders the first is Brett  Kavanaugh a 53 year-old circuit court  judge in Washington the other Amy Kony  Barrett a 46 year old who the president  recently appointed to the Seventh  Circuit in Chicago the president left  Washington on Friday to spend the  weekend at his New Jersey Resort aboard  Air Force One mr. Trump said he will  announce his nominee one week from this  Monday he told reporters that he'll be  interviewing several candidates over the  coming days the president also told  reporters he plans to bring up Russia's  interference in the 2016 election when  he meets Vladimir Putin for a summit in  Finland a little more than two weeks  from now some say the meeting holds the  promise for better relations between the  two nations others fear that mr. Trump  may embolden Putin and could drive a  wedge between the US and its European  allies here to discuss the summit is  Kimberly Martin professor of political  science at Barnard College Columbia  University Kimberly good morning good  morning thank you for having me back  thanks for coming back so in this  meeting between the two Trump or Putin  does one of them hold the advantage here  in diplomatic relations no question  Putin does because he's a very clever  man cleverness and trying to get  advantage over your opponent is  something that's really valued in  Russian political culture and I mean  he's been in that position for so long  and he's accomplished so much  internationally whereas we know that  Trump kind of got taken advantage of in  his recent summit in North Korea he  didn't seem to get much out of Kim  jong-un even if he doesn't see it that  way yeah he doesn't see it that way but  really the United States didn't get  North Korea to change much of its  behavior but the u.s. gave up military  exercises with South Korea for right now  the president says he will delve into  Russian meddling it's a topic that has  been off-limits for him for some time  the White House also said so but if  Russia denies it is that just good  enough where do you go from there it's  not clear that the summit itself can  actually accomplish much on that and we  have to remember that Obama had bought  brought up the election meddling with  Putin back in September of 2016 and  no good whatsoever because a month later  what did the Russians do they release  those Podesta emails just at a really  crucial time when Trump had been accused  of or had admitted that he had been  engaged in some behavior that didn't  make him look too savory with women and  the timing of that indicates it was a  Russian ploy and so what Obama had said  a month earlier did nothing to stop  Russians from doing that Kimberly the  president has said he has been no one's  been tougher on Russia than he has his  presidency but I think back to President  Kennedy blockading the Soviets I think  back to Jimmy Carter boycotting the  Olympics Ronald Reagan tear down this  wall  fact-check the President on that has he  been tougher than any other president in  US history and I understand that the  Soviet Union was a different country he  probably hasn't been tougher than all of  the people that you mentioned but I  think if you look at the actual policies  that Washington has put into place  we've got more troops now deployed in  Europe than we had earlier we've  increased our spending on NATO related  military purchases the sanctions have  been very strong against Russia it's not  clear that they're really accomplishing  anything and getting Russia to change  its behavior and it hasn't really come  so much from Trump as an individual a  lot of it has come from pressure that's  coming from the Senate and then from  Trump's own advisors who have not really  been all that pro-russian obviously  Syria will also be on the table in this  summit there's been some discussion that  perhaps the Russia and the u.s. can work  together in at least getting Iranian  troops out of Syria some of that  presence what can be done in regards to  Syria in these two countries well it's  really complicated and there's some  dangers about putting that forward as an  idea because then the question is what  would Putin get in return because Iran  is really a Russian Ally  Russia gets a lot of benefit out of what  Iran is doing in Syria because it means  that Russia doesn't have to be doing  even more and so if Putin says okay  we'll try to ease the Iranians out of  Syria then the question is what does the  US have to give up in response and of  course president Trump has been talking  about wanting to pull all US forces out  of Syria and if that happens our Kurdish  allies that are located in the East  might be left with no support and that  that would not be you know sort of a  good reputational thing going forward  and again as we know this is coming on  the heels this Helsinki visit on the  heels of the NATO summit yes and I mean  a Trump just in the last couple days has  been saying more anti-nato stuff again  talking about people not giving enough  money talking about how you know maybe  we're not sure that NATO is that good it  might be as bad as NAFTA in his words  and so the optics of that could be kind  of bad but again I think you know Senate  support the support of Trump's advisors  I don't think the u.s. is going to  desert NATO anytime soon all right  Kimberly Martin you thank you thank you  for having me here
 

